---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import '../styles/page-layouts.css';
import '../styles/story.css';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import freddyPhoto from '../assets/freddy-greensman.jpg';
---

<Layout
title="The Greensman on YouTube — a story — Mike Trounce"
description="Freddy's already comfortable on camera for his own Instagram. The next step is giving his business, The Greensman, a YouTube channel of its own."
>
<div class="cs-page">
<a href="/" class="back-link">&larr; Mike Trounce</a>

<header class="cs-hero">
  <Image src={freddyPhoto} alt="Freddy Trounce of The Greensman working in a client's garden" width={1170} height={768} />
  <p class="cs-caption">Freddy Trounce, The Greensman — Elstead, Surrey</p>
</header>

<span class="cs-label">Story 4</span>
<h1>The Greensman on YouTube</h1>
<p class="cs-meta">Video &amp; social media &middot; Ongoing</p>

<p class="cs-standfirst">
Freddy's already comfortable in front of a camera — he posts his own ups and downs building the business as <a href="https://www.instagram.com/trouncetalks/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">@trouncetalks</a> on Instagram. What's missing is a presence for the business itself, on the platform where a different part of his audience actually spends their time.
</p>

<section>
  <h2>Two different audiences</h2>
  <p>
  Instagram Reels are Freddy's personal story — first-person, unpolished, about him. They reach people roughly his own age. A good number of his actual and potential clients are older, don't use Instagram much, but do use YouTube — and some of them are exactly the people who hire a gardener.
  </p>
  <p>
  Rather than ask Freddy to split his personal account in two, the plan is a second channel, on a second platform, with a different subject: not Freddy's journey, but The Greensman's work.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Not starting from scratch</h2>
  <p>
  This isn't a new habit to build. Freddy's already out on jobs and quotes most days, and I've started going along to some of them, taking photos and a bit of video footage as he works. That's the same raw material his Instagram runs on — it just gets cut and framed around the business rather than around him.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Where this stands</h2>
  <p>
  The channel itself doesn't exist yet. It'll sit under Freddy's own Google account, so the business owns it outright rather than it living on mine. Next step is setting that up and publishing the first few pieces of footage already gathered on site.
  </p>
</section>

<p class="cs-foot">
<a href="/">&larr; Mike Trounce</a>
</p>
</div>
</Layout>
